{
  "rationale": "Evidence: iteration 7 (score 425.8) is the best design so far. Oval finishes 20/20 at 1.74 s with max_error 0.021 and 0.65 line-loss events. Failures are concentrated on s-bend (1/20 finished, progress 0.61, max_error about 0.062) and slalom (0/20, progress 0.12). Max error sits just above the 0.06 m off-track limit, and body_width is already at its maximum of 0.18 m, so the fix has to come from control and sensing. RMS error (0.02 to 0.03) and steering oscillation (0.2 to 0.3) are modest, so the robot is being pushed outward on sharp bends rather than ringing. The two changes that helped were forward sensor placement (iteration 3 to 7 gained about 30 points) and a higher kd (iteration 7). Widening the array (iteration 6) and raising kp with speed cuts (iteration 4) or narrowing the wheel track (iteration 5) hurt the oval, so I reject those directions. Change: move all sensors forward from y = 0.06 to y = 0.07 to continue the earlier-detection trend, and raise kd from 1.0 to 1.2 to damp overshoot on alternating bends. Everything else is unchanged (x spacing 1.5 cm, 12 mm footprints, wheel_track 0.10, mass 0.6, kp 5.0, ki 0.2, base_speed 1.2, last_direction line loss). Expected result: oval completion holds at 20/20 near 1.7 s, s-bend progress rises above 0.7 with line-loss events below 1, and slalom progress improves to above 0.2. Hypotheses: (H1) forward sensors continue to reduce line loss on bends; (H2) kd 1.2 reduces overshoot without the oscillation seen with kp 5.5. If the oval finish rate drops below 1.0, revert y to 0.06 and keep kd 1.2. If steering_oscillation rises above 0.4, reduce kd to 1.0. Measurements next: per-track completion, progress, max_error against 0.06, line_loss_events, steering_oscillation, and oval completion_time.",
